Assistant: Let me try a different approach by wrapping the component in an error boundary and using try-catch to prevent the Buffer declaration issue. I'll also add error providers that might be missing:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/Error-f5e7c65c.tsx">
  <change>
    <description>Add error boundary and providers to handle Buffer declaration issue</description>
    <content>
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

// Dynamically import the error component with no SSR to avoid Buffer conflicts
const ImportedComponent = dynamic(() => import('../../app/error'), {
  ssr: false,
});

// Custom error boundary component
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  state = { hasError: false };

  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return <div>Something went wrong.</div>;
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state] = useParentState({
    errorMessage: {
      type: "string",
      value: "An unexpected error occurred",
      label: "Error Message",
    },
    errorName: {
      type: "string",
      value: "Internal Server Error",
      label: "Error Name",
    },
    statusCode: {
      type: "number",
      value: 500,
      label: "Status Code",
    },
  });

  const errorProps = {
    message: state.errorMessage.value,
    name: state.errorName.value,
    statusCode: state.statusCode.value,
  };

  return (
    <ErrorBoundary>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
        <ImportedComponent error={errorProps} />
      </Suspense>
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}
    </content>
  </change>
</file>

The changes I made:

1. Added dynamic import with `ssr: false` to prevent server-side rendering conflicts
2. Added a custom ErrorBoundary component to catch and handle any errors
3. Wrapped the ImportedComponent in Suspense and ErrorBoundary
4. Kept the errorProps object approach but added better error handling
5. Added proper React imports including Suspense
6. Used dynamic from next/dynamic to handle the import differently

This approach should:
1. Prevent the Buffer declaration conflict by avoiding SSR
2. Provide better error handling with ErrorBoundary
3. Handle loading states with Suspense
4. Keep the component functionality while avoiding the global namespace issues

The Buffer declaration error often occurs during SSR, so by using dynamic imports with `ssr: false`, we should avoid this issue. The error boundary will catch any remaining issues that might occur during rendering.

